1
00:00:01,360 --> 00:00:02,400
Welcome to Launched.

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:10,240
I'm Charlie Chapman, and today I'm excited to bring you the prolific writer, podcaster, and recent book author, Rosemary Orchard.

3
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:24,720
Rosemary, welcome to the show.

4
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:26,240
Hi Charlie, thanks for having me.

5
00:00:26,480 --> 00:00:36,800
So we kick off the show with a little icebreaker question from the audience, and I was actually, I was starting to run low, and so I tweeted out this morning asking for some, and got quite a few, so thank you everyone who gave me some.

6
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:42,880
But one of the questions kind of kicked off a little firestorm here, and so I figured that would be a good one to go with.

7
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:46,080
So this is started by Jordan, but then a lot of people'd piled on.

8
00:00:46,240 --> 00:00:49,280
There's apparently strong opinions on this, so just a pre warning.

9
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:53,440
There's apparently a right answer, and if you're wrong, you'll be shunned.

10
00:00:54,320 --> 00:00:56,640
So I'll just read it exactly as it's written.

11
00:00:56,880 --> 00:00:59,120
Pineapples on pizza, yes or no?

12
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:07,280
Oh, I am really sorry to everyone who likes traditional pizza, but I do like pineapple on pizza.

13
00:01:07,760 --> 00:01:12,320
Okay, well I think the majority of people asking were in the same camp as you.

14
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:14,640
Well, here's my thing.

15
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:18,240
I enjoy pineapple and pizza, but I'm very aware it's not a traditional pizza.

16
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:21,200
Now, if I want a traditional pizza, then I'm not going to go with pineapple.

17
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:27,440
If I want the comfort food that I grew up with, that's ham and pineapple pizza with a lot of extra onion and cheese on top.

18
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:28,960
So it depends.

19
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:31,760
But yes, I I am a pineapple and pizza person.

20
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:37,200
Yeah, see here in the States, everyone thinks that like their pizza is traditional pizza.

21
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:41,360
But one, no one's pizza here is like traditional, you know, Italian pizza.

22
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:44,480
And everywhere you go, it's like a very different style.

23
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:48,240
In some cases, people won't even acknowledge that it's a it's really pizza.

24
00:01:48,720 --> 00:02:02,480
And where I'm from, we have a very weird pizza pizza called Saint Louis style pizza that has this kind of bizarre cheese that if you didn't grow up here, everyone hates it, but everyone who grows up here loves it, and so it's probably bad and we're all just conditioned to like it.

25
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:06,240
Well, I mean, that's probably pretty much the same as some of the food I grew up with.

26
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:13,760
My grandmother once joked that my dad could burn toast, which he has done many times, but I grew up eating it and I'm just fine.

27
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:17,280
So, you know, I learned culinary skills from the Greeks.

28
00:02:17,840 --> 00:02:18,320
There you go.

29
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:28,240
Well, if you have any questions that you'd like me to ask a guest on the show, you can tweet me at launch.fm with the hashtag icebreaker and I might ask a future guest your question.

30
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:38,960
So before we get into your book, which is the thing I'm kind of really excited to talk to you about because I don't know anything about that process, I want to give everyone a kind of a rundown of who you are.

31
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:41,920
So the question I always ask everyone is, where are you from?

32
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:45,600
Do you have any formal education related to what you're doing now?

33
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,440
And what was your career like kind of pre this project?

34
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:52,800
Although in your case we're probably going to talk about your podcast and stuff as well.

35
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:54,480
Yeah, okay.

36
00:02:54,960 --> 00:02:57,920
So, well, I'm originally from the southwest of the UK.

37
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:02,240
I was born in Bristol not that long ago, but at the same time, a very long time ago.

38
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:06,080
Depending on how you look at time, it's one of those things it's starting to sneneak up on me.

39
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:08,880
And now I live in Vienna.

40
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:14,880
And education wise and career wise, I've been a little bit all over the place.

41
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:17,200
When I was growing up, I wanted to be a teacher.

42
00:03:17,680 --> 00:03:21,920
And so I went to university and studied French and German thinking, I'll be a French and German teacher at school.

43
00:03:22,080 --> 00:03:23,120
Languages are a great idea.

44
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:28,960
And then I remembered how I had been the only person in my classes who actually liked them.

45
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:32,400
And I thought, I don't know if that's really the right thing for me.

46
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:37,040
So part of my degree program I could do I could learn to teach English as a foreign language.

47
00:03:37,360 --> 00:03:37,840
So I did that.

48
00:03:38,160 --> 00:03:44,400
And that kind of relates to what I do because a lot of what I do now is based around the idea of teaching and explaining things for other people.

49
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:47,440
And so that's been helpful.

50
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:59,520
And then after I'd been an English teacher for a while and I taught in several places, I decided that I was going to go back to university and do another degree in IT.

51
00:03:59,760 --> 00:04:01,200
Now, I didn't actually finish that degree.

52
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:06,720
I was hired while I was still studying and got a full time job and thought, well, if I've got the job, why do I need to study?

53
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:09,040
You know, these things happen.

54
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:29,760
And yeah, so I have now a general IT background, but it's not related to my day job in of podcasting and things like that in any way, the closest it comes is when people need support with setting up email certificates on their iPhones or something.

55
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:33,120
And it's like, yeah, here are the Apple instructions.

56
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:35,680
Just airdrop the certificate to your phone and you're good to go.

57
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:41,520
And they are always amazed that I know how to google, but I think every podcaster has learned that technique at some point.

58
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:46,400
Yeah, I mean, software development is basically googling is our job.

59
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:54,240
There's a meme hanging in my office at work, which is something like, doctors, don't google stuff.

60
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:55,600
That's not how we do our job.

61
00:04:56,160 --> 00:05:00,880
And then the next slide is a programmer just sitting there with their eyes going, Don't tell me how to do my job.

62
00:05:03,040 --> 00:05:18,640
I joked with my boss that seventy percent of my work is Google and Stack Overflow because it is, you know, it's finding out how to solve all these problems and finding ways that other people have failed at solving these problems so that I can solve it is, of course, another part of that.

63
00:05:19,040 --> 00:05:19,120
Yeah.

64
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:22,480
So then what is your career like then?

65
00:05:23,040 --> 00:05:31,360
Well, I'm I can't really talk about the things I'm doing at work at the moment too much because they are related to security.

66
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:32,720
Surprise, surprise, surprise.

67
00:05:33,440 --> 00:05:45,360
But essentially, I program web applications primarily and PHP scripts to run in the background to do stuff automatically when things need to be done, which is, you know, always nice.

68
00:05:45,520 --> 00:06:05,040
And that has lent itself to the other side of my life, the podcasting side of my life, really well because it's given me a deep look at how big systems work so that then I can go, okay, well, if I want to integrate with these big systems, you know, what are the little things I can do with this, which kind of led me to shortcuts, but shortcuts also led me to programming in many ways.

69
00:06:05,600 --> 00:06:10,160
So then how did you get into the I don't even know how to describe your kind of internet.

70
00:06:11,040 --> 00:06:15,760
persona because I always thought of you as a writer, but then you're also a podcaster and a author.

71
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:19,040
What was sort of the trajectory to get into that space?

72
00:06:20,400 --> 00:06:24,640
Well, I was on MacPara users as a guest a while ago.

73
00:06:25,520 --> 00:06:30,720
And I just really enjoyed being a guest and talking to David and Katie.

74
00:06:31,440 --> 00:06:33,680
It's Katie was helping host the show back then.

75
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:36,160
And I really enjoyed it.

76
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:43,840
And after that, I ended up helping moderate the MacPara users Facebook group, which has since closed down and moved to an independent forum, which is really nice.

77
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:46,640
And so I became quite embedded in the community.

78
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:57,120
And I had already had this idea of starting an automation podcast, but I was really unsure about doing a podcast all by myself and I wasn't sure I was going to be able to get guests in every week to bring people in.

79
00:06:57,280 --> 00:07:06,480
And so for weeks I was racking my brain trying to think, how do I write an email to David Sparks that doesn't sound inane that says, I want to start a podcast with you.

80
00:07:07,360 --> 00:07:08,960
Yes, I think that I can do this.

81
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:19,040
And then I kid you not, I drafted my email and I was there looking it over and I was about to hit send when a notification slides down on the top of my iPad screen.

82
00:07:19,280 --> 00:07:22,240
David Sparks, do you have some time to talk about a podcast?

83
00:07:22,480 --> 00:07:23,200
And I'm there going.

84
00:07:23,440 --> 00:07:23,600
Whoa.

85
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:30,880
Oh, that's a little freaky, but I love it because it means I don't have to send this email, which I just realized it's got a type on it.

86
00:07:31,040 --> 00:07:32,720
So heaven knows what else is wrong with that.

87
00:07:32,960 --> 00:07:35,040
Yeah, I know that feeling all too well.

88
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:38,720
Yeah, so I dropped my draft and just sent back pretty much, yeah.

89
00:07:39,440 --> 00:07:43,040
Like, I'll make time whenever the time, like, we can make this work.

90
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:45,280
And it kind of took off from there.

91
00:07:47,280 --> 00:07:54,000
And that was, I think, I started working for the suite setup, doing a little bit of writing the year before.

92
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:55,440
And that's just grown since then.

93
00:07:55,680 --> 00:08:00,880
I now have an automation slash workflow column over there every two weeks, which I really enjoy because I.

94
00:08:00,880 --> 00:08:06,640
Because I like going through the other articles and pinching things and saying, hey, like this doesn't appear to be automated.

95
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:08,160
I think this could be automated.

96
00:08:08,880 --> 00:08:09,680
Working it into stuff.

97
00:08:10,160 --> 00:08:12,000
That's the next column coming out in a couple of weeks.

98
00:08:12,320 --> 00:08:16,640
I noticed something that Mike was doing in one of his articles and was like, Huh, are you automating this?

99
00:08:16,960 --> 00:08:20,160
Mike was like, No, you're gonna make me automate it.

100
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:21,440
I'm like, Yeah, yeah, I'm.

101
00:08:21,520 --> 00:08:22,080
But it's okay.

102
00:08:22,560 --> 00:08:24,240
I'm doing it for your own good.

103
00:08:25,120 --> 00:08:26,640
And that's kind of how it all started.

104
00:08:26,880 --> 00:08:35,040
And then, yeah, I so I had Automators and then I started Nest of Folders with Scotty and I wrote Build Your Omni Focus workflow with Ryan.

105
00:08:35,840 --> 00:08:44,240
And, you know, I've been able to meet these people partially through, um, in actually both Ryan and Scotty's case because we both beta tested OnlyFocus 3.

106
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:49,360
Um, and, you know, we we were in the Slack channel on a regular basis talking to each other.

107
00:08:49,520 --> 00:08:51,280
And so it just kind of worked out.

108
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:57,920
And I'm, you know, I'm very happy that the internet exists because I wouldn't have made some of the great friends that I currently have without it.

109
00:08:58,480 --> 00:09:04,400
So like you're the thing that you seem to focus on most of the time is automation, which, you know, makes sense.

110
00:09:04,720 --> 00:09:06,240
How did you get into doing that?

111
00:09:06,480 --> 00:09:10,480
Is that just kind of an extension of your job, you know, writing applications?

112
00:09:11,280 --> 00:09:13,120
Uh, in many ways, I guess it is.

113
00:09:13,440 --> 00:09:16,480
But at the same time, um, I've always been a big fan of puzzles.

114
00:09:17,040 --> 00:09:20,640
I recently started doing jigsaw puzzles again because it's a great way to pass the time.

115
00:09:21,440 --> 00:09:25,360
And it's a nice thing that you can do with your hands so you don't sit there just eating all the time.

116
00:09:26,560 --> 00:09:28,560
It's the only other alternative to do with your hands.

117
00:09:29,200 --> 00:09:29,280
Yeah.

118
00:09:29,840 --> 00:09:30,400
Yeah, exactly.

119
00:09:30,640 --> 00:09:33,200
So like reading, it doesn't really occupy both my hands.

120
00:09:33,360 --> 00:09:35,760
I can still use one hand to eat chocolate or popcorn or something.

121
00:09:35,920 --> 00:09:39,680
It's like, I need something that occupies both hands here and my whole brain.

122
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:43,920
Because I'm not very good when my brain's not doing something.

123
00:09:44,240 --> 00:09:46,080
I can't sit there in silence for a long time.

124
00:09:47,120 --> 00:09:48,960
Unless, you know, I have a problem to work on.

125
00:09:49,200 --> 00:09:51,200
And that's really what automation is for me.

126
00:09:51,280 --> 00:09:53,840
And I'm looking at things going, oh, this feels kind of clumsy.

127
00:09:53,920 --> 00:09:54,800
And, oh, I don't know.

128
00:09:55,680 --> 00:09:57,360
Like the air table app on iOS.

129
00:09:57,920 --> 00:09:58,080
Why?

130
00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:01,360
Like, you know, I can't easily add stuff and it takes a long time.

131
00:10:01,520 --> 00:10:05,360
And I want to be able to add ten ideas in a go and then sort them all out afterwards.

132
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:11,600
And that's where my love for automation really comes in because it's puzzles that I'm solving.

133
00:10:12,160 --> 00:10:16,240
And sometimes it's just different ways to do things or it's just ways to optimize my workflows.

134
00:10:16,400 --> 00:10:23,840
I recently rechecked a lot of the workflows that I've been using for screencasts online so that everything is just automatically there for me in drafts.

135
00:10:24,080 --> 00:10:32,320
And I've been playing with beta applications, automation, beta applications, the pushcut pages specifically and the server feature to get that working.

136
00:10:32,640 --> 00:10:39,600
And I just really like that sense of accomplishment when you've not only solved a puzzle, but you've solved it in a way that really makes your life easier.

137
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,080
Yeah, I imagine that's like really satisfying.

138
00:10:43,120 --> 00:10:51,920
I'm definitely the non normal developer who kind of just goes with the extremely manual, slow way of doing things for way too long.

139
00:10:53,120 --> 00:11:05,040
It's not until, like, you or, you know, someone makes a, like, post about a shortcut that they made and I'm like, oh, that's a thing that I've been manually doing for like a year that takes me, you know, twenty minutes and I could do that in four seconds.

140
00:11:05,200 --> 00:11:05,680
That would be nice.

141
00:11:07,440 --> 00:11:13,760
And I think that, you know, it sometimes takes a very specific mindset to be like, yeah, you could automate this.

142
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,640
So I recently helped my mom automate something.

143
00:11:17,040 --> 00:11:19,840
She has been driving to the train station, having to park her car.

144
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:26,800
And the way that you park your car and pay for the parking at this train station is you have to text a specific number with your registration plate information.

145
00:11:27,760 --> 00:11:29,120
And you know what she was doing?

146
00:11:29,520 --> 00:11:32,640
She was opening messages, starting a new message.

147
00:11:32,880 --> 00:11:37,280
She was finding the number, typing in the right information and then pressing send.

148
00:11:37,840 --> 00:11:41,520
And I was there going, okay, mom, I love you.

149
00:11:42,320 --> 00:11:44,720
Here's a button on your home screen that does this.

150
00:11:46,560 --> 00:11:51,280
And she thought that I had done some kind of witchcraft.

151
00:11:51,360 --> 00:11:53,520
There was definitely the word hack somewhere in there.

152
00:11:54,640 --> 00:11:56,320
And then she said, This is brilliant.

153
00:11:56,560 --> 00:11:57,040
Thank you very much.

154
00:11:57,200 --> 00:12:04,960
Because she's typed her registration plate information wrong a couple of times and they still charge you even if you've given the wrong registration plate and getting refunds from this company is a nightmare.

155
00:12:05,680 --> 00:12:06,960
And so I automated it.

156
00:12:07,040 --> 00:12:08,880
And then she was like, But what if I take the other car?

157
00:12:09,040 --> 00:12:12,400
Because my mom and my dad both have cars and occasionally she takes my dad's car.

158
00:12:12,480 --> 00:12:15,600
And I was like, Okay, here's a slight modification.

159
00:12:15,920 --> 00:12:18,560
Now it will show you a choice of the two registration plates.

160
00:12:18,800 --> 00:12:20,640
Let's license plateate in British.

161
00:12:21,920 --> 00:12:25,600
So, yeah, and that was just something that I was there going, immediately.

162
00:12:25,760 --> 00:12:31,120
Well, of course you can automate that, but most people don't see those opportunities, you know, because they're not looking for them.

163
00:12:31,600 --> 00:12:41,040
They're not used to saying, Oh, there must be a easier way to do this until something is really, really bugging them, and I try to avoid things bugging me in the first place.

164
00:12:42,720 --> 00:12:45,760
So, you already talked about your podcast a little bit.

165
00:12:45,920 --> 00:12:49,200
So, you have Automators with David Sparks that you were talking about just a minute ago.

166
00:12:49,360 --> 00:12:49,440
Yeah.

167
00:12:49,680 --> 00:12:52,560
And then you have another one called Nested Folders with Scotty Jackson.

168
00:12:53,200 --> 00:12:53,200
Yeah.

169
00:12:53,600 --> 00:12:53,680
Yeah.

170
00:12:53,920 --> 00:12:57,680
That's a productivity based with a slight GTD joke in in the title.

171
00:12:59,040 --> 00:13:04,400
Yeah, I'm not as into GTD world as some people, so that one went over my head.

172
00:13:05,280 --> 00:13:05,600
That's okay.

173
00:13:05,920 --> 00:13:09,600
It still kind of sounds productivity related if you're there like folders, nested folders.

174
00:13:09,760 --> 00:13:11,120
Yeah, I guess that could be productivity.

175
00:13:11,680 --> 00:13:12,240
Yeah, yeah.

176
00:13:12,640 --> 00:13:16,160
And so Automators is part of the Relay FM network, right?

177
00:13:16,880 --> 00:13:16,960
Yeah.

178
00:13:17,440 --> 00:13:19,760
And then nested folders is completely independent?

179
00:13:20,400 --> 00:13:20,880
Yeah, it is.

180
00:13:21,200 --> 00:13:30,400
So Scotty and I are just doing that on our own and we're really enjoying it and trying to figure out exactly where it's going longer term because we don't want to stop doing it any time soon.

181
00:13:31,040 --> 00:13:31,760
So I'm curious.

182
00:13:32,160 --> 00:13:46,160
I mean, obviously, I'mm sure there are details you can't talk about or whatever, but I'm curious how the difference is between being part of a network with all that support and then doing something on your own, like, how do those two projects feel different in that way?

183
00:13:46,880 --> 00:13:52,560
Well, I'm very fortunate because not only is Automator's on a network, it's on real AFM.

184
00:13:52,800 --> 00:14:02,240
Now, I will admit, I've never been on another podcast network, but I do know that Mike and Steven do a lot of work to make the whole process really, really easy for us.

185
00:14:03,040 --> 00:14:10,800
You know, they have a designer and editors and assistants and things like that, which, you know, all help everything to just run smoothly.

186
00:14:11,920 --> 00:14:25,760
And so nested folders in many ways is a lot more complex, but at the same time, knowing what I already knew about how Relay FM works, allowed me to set up some systems for nested folders, which automate a lot of the background process.

187
00:14:26,080 --> 00:14:33,920
So, for example, whenever one episode goes up, it automatically creates a draft blog post in the WordPress blog for the next episode ready for that one.

188
00:14:34,160 --> 00:14:39,680
So, whenever that one is edited and ready to go up, you know that the outline is already there in WordPress.

189
00:14:40,480 --> 00:14:56,960
And that makes it easier in many ways, but it is obviously different to do things without the financial backing of a network and all the automatic, for want of a better word, advertising that comes with the fact that you're on a network with other podcasts that people listen to.

190
00:14:57,360 --> 00:14:57,520
Right.

191
00:14:58,320 --> 00:14:58,400
Yeah.

192
00:14:58,720 --> 00:15:04,800
And do you feel like any less pressure though since it's just kind of your own thing where you can kind of do whatever you want?

193
00:15:05,440 --> 00:15:09,680
Um, no, but that's mostly because Steven and Mike never met each other.

194
00:15:09,920 --> 00:15:10,800
I should never have said anything.

195
00:15:11,040 --> 00:15:11,760
I mean, that's it.

196
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:16,320
Of course, Relay Fam has high quality standards that they want everything to adhere to.

197
00:15:16,560 --> 00:15:17,280
Um, that's it.

198
00:15:17,680 --> 00:15:21,440
I've never, you know, had to talk to them about that sort of thing.

199
00:15:21,840 --> 00:15:23,280
It's just understood.

200
00:15:24,080 --> 00:15:27,920
I am someone who wants to do things as well as possible anyway.

201
00:15:28,320 --> 00:15:40,480
So even though there might not be an external body, you know, saying, Oh yeah, you know, your audio has to be done exactly like this and that and the other for Nest of Folders, you know, it's still we want it to sound good.

202
00:15:41,520 --> 00:15:49,120
And, you know, it's like if people go back and listen to the very first few episodes and the audio quality on Scotty's side is not great, but we got that sorted really quickly.

203
00:15:49,600 --> 00:15:52,640
And it's just, you know, been quite easy from there.

204
00:15:53,760 --> 00:15:58,320
But, you know, we don't have, you know, the other people to go and bounce ideas off if we need to.

205
00:15:59,040 --> 00:16:01,520
But at the same time, we're doing, I feel like we're doing okay.

206
00:16:02,160 --> 00:16:19,440
Yeah, and I guess I wasn't really meaning from the network itself, but more from the being part of, like, I mean, I don't know your numbers, but I assume Automators has a bigger audience and there's sort of regular, you know, advertisers that you have to kind of manage and all that stuff.

207
00:16:19,760 --> 00:16:27,280
Yeah, so yeah, the audience numbers are definitely bigger for Automators, and I think a lot of that's down to David, who, my co host, who does a great job.

208
00:16:28,240 --> 00:16:37,360
And, you know, people have been listening to David for a long time now because he's been doing MacPower users for over ten years, which is amazing, and I'm very happy to be working with him on that.

209
00:16:38,480 --> 00:16:47,200
But so in some ways the fact that Ness of Folders is smaller does make it less pressure, but it also means in some ways there's more pressure because of course we want to grow those numbers.

210
00:16:47,520 --> 00:16:50,880
And I mean it is smaller, but it's by no means a small podcast.

211
00:16:51,680 --> 00:16:59,840
You know, I was looking at it and looking at what Lipson say are the average deaths for podcasts and going, okay, well, that our numbers are bigger than those.

212
00:17:00,160 --> 00:17:00,720
Oh, okay.

213
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:02,560
That's impressive.

214
00:17:02,800 --> 00:17:04,240
I feel quite good about myself now.

215
00:17:05,280 --> 00:17:09,760
Which was also secretly terrifying because it's like, okay, wow, all these people are listening.

216
00:17:10,080 --> 00:17:13,280
And I'm kind of just making this setup up as I go along.

217
00:17:14,160 --> 00:17:15,120
It does seem to be working.

218
00:17:15,760 --> 00:17:17,040
So that's awesome.

219
00:17:17,440 --> 00:17:32,960
And I guess like the fact that you started with Automators, you got to sort of learn, I assume that was like a big learning opportunity, uh, both because of the like assistance you got from Relay, but also just working with David who's been doing this, like you said, forever and very successful at it.

220
00:17:33,680 --> 00:17:33,680
Yes.

221
00:17:34,080 --> 00:17:38,640
Um, and, and David started out with, okay, like, this is what I want to do for this, this and this.

222
00:17:38,880 --> 00:17:40,240
And I was like, okay, great.

223
00:17:40,800 --> 00:17:44,240
That means I don't need to make decisions because decisions take a lot of mental overhead.

224
00:17:44,400 --> 00:17:46,400
So I try to get rid of those as much as I possibly can.

225
00:17:46,880 --> 00:17:52,880
Um, and so the fact that someone was like, this is how I do things on my power users at the time it was free agents, it's now focused..

226
00:17:53,040 --> 00:17:54,800
It was like, okay, great, cool.

227
00:17:55,360 --> 00:17:56,400
Then we're doing it like that.

228
00:17:56,640 --> 00:17:58,320
Let's, you know, keep your systems in place.

229
00:17:58,560 --> 00:18:00,720
And I've been able to take some of that and use it.

230
00:18:00,880 --> 00:18:07,840
So, for example, at the start of every episode, David or I count down from three and then on zero, we'll both press the record button at the same time.

231
00:18:07,920 --> 00:18:14,720
And I've taken that to nested folders because it does make the editing a bit easier if the audio starts at the same time.

232
00:18:15,520 --> 00:18:19,840
And, you know, just little tips and tricks like that have been fascinating to learn.

233
00:18:20,400 --> 00:18:25,200
And, uh, of course, being able to ask Marco Armit for a microphone recommendation is always an expensive activity.

234
00:18:26,320 --> 00:18:27,520
It's an unfair advantage.

235
00:18:27,760 --> 00:18:30,480
Although he, I guess he posts all that online too now.

236
00:18:30,800 --> 00:18:32,960
Yeah, yeah, he posts all that on his blog.

237
00:18:33,920 --> 00:18:38,000
And at some point I did ask, you know, really people, which microphone should I buy?

238
00:18:38,400 --> 00:18:43,520
There was basically consensus, just get, I think it was a Shure 87a, and that's what I'm using now.

239
00:18:43,680 --> 00:18:44,640
So there you go.

240
00:18:45,440 --> 00:18:45,440
Yeah.

241
00:18:46,080 --> 00:18:54,880
So let's move on to your book, because I don't know that I really know anyone who's actually written a full book before, and you've written two.

242
00:18:55,440 --> 00:19:01,280
So the first one you have is called Build Your Omni Focus Workflow, and you coauthored that with Ryan Dotson.

243
00:19:01,840 --> 00:19:02,400
Yeah, yeah.

244
00:19:02,480 --> 00:19:05,840
So Ryan was a colleague Omni Focus 3 beta tester like myself.

245
00:19:06,720 --> 00:19:12,480
And we both thought, hey, you know what would be really interesting that we both would enjoy writing a book about omni focus.

246
00:19:12,800 --> 00:19:14,240
And so we did that collaboratively.

247
00:19:14,400 --> 00:19:19,920
At the time he was living in Japan and I'm here in Austria, so we had time zone differences but also overlap.

248
00:19:21,120 --> 00:19:23,760
And that was, you know, great fun to do.

249
00:19:24,720 --> 00:19:30,960
And it was good to work with him because he's an English teacher, a former English teacher as well.

250
00:19:31,760 --> 00:19:34,560
And his grammar standards are considerably better than mine.

251
00:19:35,120 --> 00:19:38,480
So I caused him many a migraine with my errand comma usage.

252
00:19:38,800 --> 00:19:44,080
I do like to just randomly scatter them all over the page and not near where you would actually expect a comma comma to appear.

253
00:19:44,320 --> 00:19:47,280
I tend to just write and then try and figure everything out later.

254
00:19:48,320 --> 00:19:52,000
And so poor Ryan had to deal with my poor grammar there.

255
00:19:52,960 --> 00:19:55,760
And that was really great to work with Ryan on that.

256
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:57,680
And we did that totally independently.

257
00:19:57,840 --> 00:19:58,800
We did that all on our own.

258
00:19:59,360 --> 00:20:05,840
The only help we got was we got someone else to design the book cover, Josh Hughes, who has also done a lot of omnifocus perspective icons.

259
00:20:06,960 --> 00:20:13,760
Because we both, Ryan and I both agreed, we could do artwork, but it might not be great.

260
00:20:15,200 --> 00:20:18,720
So get someone else who actually knows what they're doing there to do that for us.

261
00:20:18,880 --> 00:20:24,880
Well, and it's it's a cool I love love that kind of rainbow dots with the check mark sort of inversed in there.

262
00:20:25,520 --> 00:20:29,680
Yeah, well, we just gave Josh a couple of ideas and that's what he came back with.

263
00:20:29,840 --> 00:20:34,720
And he actually made the podcast artwork for Nested Boulders for us as well, which was really nice of him.

264
00:20:34,960 --> 00:20:35,680
So nice.

265
00:20:36,160 --> 00:20:41,280
So how did you go about making this, like, had you ever done any technical documentation before?

266
00:20:43,760 --> 00:20:51,600
So I used to work in the service center actually in the university that I worked for before I became a programmer there, I was working in the service center.

267
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:57,600
And a lot of that involved, in my case, actually translating documentation and making Mac specific documentation.

268
00:20:59,120 --> 00:21:04,720
Because when I started there, there was another person who had a Mac as well, but he left shortly after I joined.

269
00:21:05,760 --> 00:21:14,560
And so they needed people to test that everything was actually working on the Mac as you would expect it to, and also to take the screenshots and write up the documentation.

270
00:21:15,600 --> 00:21:25,600
And so I had to go through and translate all of the existing documentation for everything else into not just English but decent English because there were some interesting Google Translate bits in there before.

271
00:21:26,080 --> 00:21:27,680
So I went through and cleared all that up.

272
00:21:27,840 --> 00:21:31,040
And it's like, okay, you've got about eight thousand ways of saying connection.

273
00:21:31,120 --> 00:21:36,000
And I'm just going to use the word connection in English because German can be quite wordy in some ways.

274
00:21:36,160 --> 00:21:38,960
They have many words for the same thing, especially finance.

275
00:21:39,360 --> 00:21:40,560
Finance is not just finance.

276
00:21:40,880 --> 00:21:43,680
There's like eight thousand different kinds of economics.

277
00:21:44,560 --> 00:21:51,840
And so that translates generally quite well to all of the technical sphere where you're there going, okay, there are lots of different words that are being used here.

278
00:21:52,240 --> 00:21:53,680
English doesn't have these nuances.

279
00:21:54,640 --> 00:22:10,160
Okay, let's just keep it simple, you know, and bear in mind that a lot of the people reading this documentation will not necessarily be speaking English as a first language, which was something that we also tried to keep in mind in Build Your Omni Focus workflow, that a lot of people wouldn't be speaking English as a native language.

280
00:22:10,800 --> 00:22:16,800
And so they would be reading these things and we wanted to not make them reach for a dictionary every three words or something.

281
00:22:17,360 --> 00:22:17,600
Interesting.

282
00:22:17,760 --> 00:22:22,720
So it's like English is the kind of intermediary language that more people have access to.

283
00:22:23,520 --> 00:22:24,560
Yes, exactly.

284
00:22:25,280 --> 00:22:32,480
Especially the universities here in Austria, they get a lot of Italian students because Italy is so close.

285
00:22:33,360 --> 00:22:35,200
And of course, you have Erasmus students.

286
00:22:35,440 --> 00:22:38,400
That's the European essentially university exchange program.

287
00:22:38,800 --> 00:22:47,040
And so you have students from around Europe and English is the de facto, you know, lingua franca, slightly ironically for the French.

288
00:22:49,520 --> 00:23:00,320
And so there were a lot of international students and also we would have master students from all over the world, because Austria is relatively cheap to live and study in and they've got a good education system here.

289
00:23:01,040 --> 00:23:07,520
So you'd have a lot of students and they wouldn't necessarily speak English as a first language and most of them were still learning German.

290
00:23:07,920 --> 00:23:10,400
Their German may not have been as good as their English.

291
00:23:10,560 --> 00:23:14,880
So having things like technical instructions available in English definitely made life easier.

292
00:23:15,280 --> 00:23:20,560
And it certainly meant that I actually understood how all of our systems worked, which was a very good side benefit.

293
00:23:20,800 --> 00:23:29,840
Yeah, I have to imagine like whoever is doing technical docs for any system is probably the person who understands this better than anyone, probably including the people who wrote the system in the first place.

294
00:23:30,720 --> 00:23:41,440
You say that, but every so often I come across documentation, you know, things I did, you know, back when I was working in the service center, and I look at it and go, I have absolutely no idea what I meant there, but I'm pretty certain that's wrong.

295
00:23:42,240 --> 00:23:42,320
See?

296
00:23:42,960 --> 00:23:55,040
Well, I do that with my code for usually a day or a week old code, so that's probably No, this morning I got to work and I looked at something I wrote yesterday and I went, I have absolutely no idea why I did this or what I did.

297
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,480
And more importantly, I have no idea why this works.

298
00:23:59,280 --> 00:24:01,200
Yeah, that's always a scary realization.

299
00:24:01,680 --> 00:24:02,160
Yeah, yeah.

300
00:24:02,560 --> 00:24:07,120
I I left it alone and it did indeed blow up later in the day, but I found out why and fixed it properly.

301
00:24:08,800 --> 00:24:15,200
So, so you had some experience with technical documentation, but how did you go about building a big book?

302
00:24:16,320 --> 00:24:20,400
Like what apps did you use and this especially in this case you were working with another person.

303
00:24:21,040 --> 00:24:33,200
Yeah, so Ryan and I started out with a mind node document on my site and then, you know, we shared it back and forth and looked at it and tried to figure out what apps we were going to use to write it.

304
00:24:33,760 --> 00:24:39,360
And then we realized that Ryan didn't have mind node and his mind node trial was coming to an end and he was like, well, I don't really know.

305
00:24:39,360 --> 00:24:41,360
He was there going, well, I don't really want to buy it if I'm just going to be using it for the book.

306
00:24:41,600 --> 00:24:52,640
It's like, well, this is probably a good opportunity to move our document into Omni Outliner, which is of course a much more linear approach to outlining a book, because we knew that we were going to need some kind of ordering in there at some point.

307
00:24:52,880 --> 00:24:57,360
And while you can do that in a mind map form, it's just not as strict.

308
00:24:57,680 --> 00:24:57,840
Right.

309
00:24:58,880 --> 00:25:01,120
By definition, it's more like loose.

310
00:25:01,760 --> 00:25:01,840
Yeah.

311
00:25:02,720 --> 00:25:09,760
So Ryan and I switched over to Omni Outliner and did some messing around there and got things, you know, roughly ordered and tried to figure it out.

312
00:25:10,560 --> 00:25:12,880
And then we tried to decide how we were going to write the book.

313
00:25:13,120 --> 00:25:22,080
And at one point we had this idea of, well, we'll write it in Mark markdown files and store them in GitHub so that we can each check it out and pull requests and things like that.

314
00:25:22,960 --> 00:25:27,760
And then I said, Wait, have we thought about using Scrivener?

315
00:25:28,960 --> 00:25:34,960
And Ryan had a look at Scrivener and said, Well, it seems like it was made for writing books, which it is.

316
00:25:35,840 --> 00:25:37,760
And then, Well, we should probably just use this.

317
00:25:38,320 --> 00:25:41,600
Now, for people who don't know Scrivener, it's an app.

318
00:25:41,760 --> 00:25:43,760
It's written by a company called Literature and Latte.

319
00:25:44,240 --> 00:25:53,920
And the original creator of the app, who still maintains it and works on it to this day, wrote it because he was writing a book and he couldn't find any good good software to do it.

320
00:25:54,560 --> 00:25:58,880
Now, the only catch with Scrivener is it is not meant for multiperson editing.

321
00:25:59,920 --> 00:26:08,640
It is a strictly one device use at a time, which meant that Ryan and I were having to send each other iMessages of Hey, can you exit the book, please?

322
00:26:08,640 --> 00:26:09,600
I want to do something in it.

323
00:26:10,080 --> 00:26:11,920
And Rose, you left the book open again.

324
00:26:12,080 --> 00:26:16,240
Please, can you tell me that you haven't fallen asleep so I can actually do some work on it this morning?

325
00:26:17,680 --> 00:26:18,640
That one came a few times.

326
00:26:18,960 --> 00:26:22,640
So you just had like a single file in like Dropbox or iCloud or something?

327
00:26:22,960 --> 00:26:23,520
Yeah, exactly.

328
00:26:23,760 --> 00:26:26,640
We had this single file in Dropbox syncing back and forth between us.

329
00:26:27,360 --> 00:26:31,200
And then inside of Scrivener, you can color the code the files.

330
00:26:32,320 --> 00:26:37,760
And so we had a system of color coding of light color.

331
00:26:38,080 --> 00:26:39,760
So Brian was green and I was purple.

332
00:26:40,320 --> 00:26:42,400
So the light color would be I'm working on this.

333
00:26:42,640 --> 00:26:45,360
The dark color would be Hey, can you please review this?

334
00:26:46,080 --> 00:26:49,680
And then I believe we had some kind of brown color, which is I have no idea what we're doing here.

335
00:26:49,760 --> 00:26:51,280
We should probably talk about this at some point.

336
00:26:52,880 --> 00:26:54,960
And there was definitely a color for that.

337
00:26:55,120 --> 00:26:56,560
I can't remember if it was brown or not.

338
00:26:56,800 --> 00:26:57,360
I think it was.

339
00:26:59,600 --> 00:27:01,840
And we just went from there.

340
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:05,200
So we went through and looked at things and called dibs on bits and so on.

341
00:27:06,400 --> 00:27:11,760
And yeah, I just wrote chunks and went through the bits that the other person had written.

342
00:27:12,320 --> 00:27:21,200
And then at the end, we converted it to PDF and went through it on our iPads with Apple Pencils to try and find spelling mistakes and things like that.

343
00:27:21,440 --> 00:27:29,040
And you know, widows and orphans, as they're called topographically, where you've got just one sentence on a page or something like that.

344
00:27:30,560 --> 00:27:32,080
Or just the title is on a page.

345
00:27:33,520 --> 00:27:37,360
And then the actual content of the chapter is on the next page and fixed all that.

346
00:27:37,760 --> 00:27:40,400
And yeah, it somehow worked.

347
00:27:40,640 --> 00:27:44,160
And I'm still quite impressed that nothing actually worked.

348
00:27:44,160 --> 00:27:44,720
Nothing actually blew up on us.

349
00:27:44,960 --> 00:27:55,920
We did encounter a couple of collisions in Scrivener where one of us had opened the document and left it open, the other person had opened it and not read the warning that it was open on another device and just kept working.

350
00:27:57,040 --> 00:28:02,640
But they were all merged back together successfully and no serious work was lost, which is always good.

351
00:28:02,960 --> 00:28:03,200
Nice.

352
00:28:03,440 --> 00:28:05,040
So how long did you end up working on that?

353
00:28:05,440 --> 00:28:05,840
Oh gosh.

354
00:28:06,080 --> 00:28:07,600
I mean, we launched it just before Christmas.

355
00:28:07,920 --> 00:28:10,960
I think we started working in September, so three months or so.

356
00:28:11,520 --> 00:28:14,000
Wait, that was Christmas of last year?

357
00:28:14,880 --> 00:28:15,440
The year before.

358
00:28:15,840 --> 00:28:16,560
Okay, that's what I thought.

359
00:28:16,800 --> 00:28:16,880
Okay.

360
00:28:17,040 --> 00:28:20,240
I was like, Man, did you get two books written in that short amount of time?

361
00:28:20,480 --> 00:28:20,560
No.

362
00:28:20,640 --> 00:28:21,840
No, no, gosh, no.

363
00:28:22,800 --> 00:28:27,120
If I had done that, I would have actually had a nervous breakdown instead of just thinking I was going to have one a couple of times.

364
00:28:28,480 --> 00:28:31,120
But yeah, and then we had to coordinate the launch.

365
00:28:31,680 --> 00:28:40,560
Originally, we had planned to do it us time wise, but Ryan is in Japan, and for people who don't know, you know, Japan's asleep and the US is awake pretty much.

366
00:28:41,760 --> 00:28:45,840
And so that would have involved either him getting up really early or staying up really late.

367
00:28:46,000 --> 00:28:53,040
So we decided that we would just launch it while we were both awake, and that way if there were any significant problems, there were two people around to deal with it, rather than one.

368
00:28:54,080 --> 00:28:58,080
And it turned out there were no significant problems, so we kind of sat there in awful.

369
00:28:58,080 --> 00:29:02,400
And all while, you know, people slowly bought the book and went, oh, wow, this is scary, but in a good way.

370
00:29:02,960 --> 00:29:04,000
So how did you launch it?

371
00:29:04,160 --> 00:29:05,680
Like, how were you even selling the book?

372
00:29:06,320 --> 00:29:14,400
So I just threw together a quick WordPress site with WooCommerce and a theme that I had bought a couple of years before that was still getting updates.

373
00:29:15,040 --> 00:29:19,920
It's based on Genesis, which is a framework theme in WordPress.

374
00:29:21,200 --> 00:29:26,240
And so we just put WooCommerce in there, set it up with Stripe and PayPal for payments.

375
00:29:27,600 --> 00:29:29,520
And yeah, did that.

376
00:29:30,320 --> 00:29:31,120
And it worked.

377
00:29:31,840 --> 00:29:34,800
So we had to do a couple of test runs, but fortunately.

378
00:29:35,280 --> 00:29:42,880
Stripe and PayPal both have test credentials that you can use where they won't actually charge you, which is good because I would otherwise I would have had to buy my own book several times.

379
00:29:45,200 --> 00:29:47,120
Just giving Stripe the hero like 10 percent.

380
00:29:47,600 --> 00:29:49,040
Yeah, I think it's like 3 percent actually.

381
00:29:49,200 --> 00:29:49,760
It's not that much.

382
00:29:49,920 --> 00:29:50,000
Yeah.

383
00:29:50,320 --> 00:29:50,720
Or 3.

384
00:29:50,880 --> 00:29:50,880
Yeah.

385
00:29:51,040 --> 00:29:54,320
I'm in Apple numbers, so Yeah, so Apple's 30 percent.

386
00:29:55,040 --> 00:30:03,680
And we also set it up with Smashwords and Smashwords allowed us to submit to Apple and a couple of other places like we're on the Kobo store and so on.

387
00:30:04,160 --> 00:30:05,760
And we had wanted to sell through Amazon.

388
00:30:06,960 --> 00:30:12,000
But Amazon wants a really large amount of revenue if your book is over something like 10 or 15 dollars.

389
00:30:12,000 --> 00:30:12,320
Something like ten or fifteen dollars.

390
00:30:12,480 --> 00:30:13,760
I think they want like eighty percent.

391
00:30:14,880 --> 00:30:16,080
And so, yeah.

392
00:30:16,320 --> 00:30:25,440
So we decided that, you know, like as much as we would love people to give the option to buy it directly on their Kindle and just download it from Amazon, we don't like that option that much.

393
00:30:25,760 --> 00:30:32,560
So if you buy it from us, you get all three formats and you can just send it to your Kindle via the secret e-mail address.

394
00:30:32,720 --> 00:30:36,480
Yeah, I was about to say, can you send, you can send files manually over to the Kindle?

395
00:30:36,800 --> 00:30:37,520
Yeah, yeah, you can.

396
00:30:37,760 --> 00:30:37,840
Okay.

397
00:30:38,480 --> 00:30:38,960
That makes sense.

398
00:30:39,200 --> 00:30:39,280
Yeah.

399
00:30:39,520 --> 00:30:46,320
So we suggest that as a workaround for people because I have a feeling most people wouldn't want us to give eighty percent to Amazon either.

400
00:30:47,600 --> 00:30:49,120
Yeah, that's unexpected..

401
00:30:49,200 --> 00:30:49,920
I didn't realize that.

402
00:30:50,080 --> 00:30:53,040
Yeah, it makes Apple's 30% sound really reasonable, doesn't it?

403
00:30:53,280 --> 00:30:54,080
Yeah, no kidding.

404
00:30:55,200 --> 00:30:56,320
Definitely could be worse.

405
00:30:57,280 --> 00:31:01,200
And so then last year, you came out with a book all on your own.

406
00:31:01,360 --> 00:31:05,920
And this was the Take Control of Automation on the Go for iOS and iPadOS.

407
00:31:06,240 --> 00:31:08,240
Yeah, I can't take all the credit.

408
00:31:08,400 --> 00:31:12,720
So it's my name on the front cover, but Joe Kissel at Take Control was both my editor and the publisher.

409
00:31:13,120 --> 00:31:17,840
And honestly, you know, like, I needed someone else there going, Commas?

410
00:31:18,640 --> 00:31:20,320
Are they entirely optional?

411
00:31:20,640 --> 00:31:23,680
Or what about consistent spelling or just spelling?

412
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:26,080
Have you heard of spell check every so now and then.

413
00:31:26,560 --> 00:31:33,760
And I pity Joe for what he had to go through with this, especially because I had to write in American English, which I'm not very good at, it turns out.

414
00:31:34,400 --> 00:31:35,520
That was really hard to do.

415
00:31:35,840 --> 00:31:38,640
Now, American English just meaning like the spelling of words like color.

416
00:31:39,120 --> 00:31:40,320
Yeah, not just spelling though.

417
00:31:40,480 --> 00:31:41,440
There's a bunch of grammar.

418
00:31:42,160 --> 00:31:47,440
So, for example, write in American English, you would just say, Oh yeah, I'll write him.

419
00:31:48,080 --> 00:31:53,680
For me, as a British English speaker, I'm there going, you're missing a word here, you're missing the two, please add two.

420
00:31:54,000 --> 00:31:56,000
You write to someone, you don't write them.

421
00:31:56,880 --> 00:32:00,320
You know, you're not writing a person here, you're writing to a person.

422
00:32:01,440 --> 00:32:02,000
And stuff like that.

423
00:32:02,560 --> 00:32:24,960
So there were a whole bunch of things in there and just little things and especially the formatting was a little tricky because the program that we use allowed us to automatically create the ePub and the Mobi and the PDF, but that meant that you had to write in rich text and I am so used to writing in markdown format and converting everything to rich text later and adding formatting that that was quite tricky.

424
00:32:25,440 --> 00:32:31,440
And fortunately I was able to set up a bunch of keyboard shortcuts to help me with that, but every so often I go through and look at it and go, huh?

425
00:32:32,080 --> 00:32:35,920
So it turns out just wrapping words in underscores doesn't automatically make it italic.

426
00:32:37,840 --> 00:32:43,040
And so this was through Take Control books, which is like a book publishing company, is that right?

427
00:32:43,440 --> 00:32:43,520
Yes.

428
00:32:44,320 --> 00:32:52,240
Take Control are a publishing company specifically take control of Mac and IOS related things, and they're based actually in California as well.

429
00:32:53,760 --> 00:32:58,160
A lot of my life seems to be based in the US at the moment, which is intriguing for somebody who lives in Austria.

430
00:32:59,440 --> 00:33:07,920
But yes, so they publish a whole series of things, and Joe, the publisher, is actually the most prolific author, and he took it over a while ago, actually.

431
00:33:08,320 --> 00:33:11,040
So how did that how did that whole thing get going?

432
00:33:11,280 --> 00:33:13,440
Like, did you approach them or did they approach you?

433
00:33:13,760 --> 00:33:21,600
Yeah, I approached Joe because he had sent out a survey to do a survey to take control of customers a while before asking you what you wanted to see a book about.

434
00:33:22,160 --> 00:33:26,640
Now, of course, I didn't put down that I wanted to see a book about shortcuts because I already know about shortcuts.

435
00:33:27,680 --> 00:33:30,880
And then I was looking through the lineup going, well, do they have a book about shortcuts?

436
00:33:31,040 --> 00:33:32,640
Maybe I could learn something from it if they do.

437
00:33:34,000 --> 00:33:37,920
And I couldn't find one, so I emailed Joe and he got back to me very quickly.

438
00:33:38,000 --> 00:33:44,560
And then we talked and he said, well, it's not that I don't want a book about shortcuts, it's just that I think that not that many people are going to buy it.

439
00:33:45,440 --> 00:33:53,520
And that, you know, that's a poor bet for both of us because you'll have to invest a bunch of time, we'll invest money and, you know, we won't really do very well.

440
00:33:54,400 --> 00:33:57,840
And then he did some more thinking about it and said, you know what, I think actually we can make this work.

441
00:33:58,640 --> 00:34:03,760
And so we went through and I did an outline and the process for me is pretty much always the same.

442
00:34:03,920 --> 00:34:10,080
I start with a mind map, then I switch to an outline and then I transfer it into the writing program.

443
00:34:10,320 --> 00:34:11,600
Now, usually I would use drafts.

444
00:34:13,120 --> 00:34:17,440
For take control, we use Mysis Writer Pro, which a lot of people have never heard of.

445
00:34:18,000 --> 00:34:19,840
It's an old program, but it's a very good program.

446
00:34:20,880 --> 00:34:27,040
If occasionally perspicy, it has opinions about how things should be done and then that's how you do it.

447
00:34:27,600 --> 00:34:29,600
And I just got on with writing in there.

448
00:34:31,280 --> 00:34:38,080
Now, of course, this meant I couldn't write on iOS, which is a shame because I was writing about iOS.

449
00:34:38,400 --> 00:34:58,320
So I would be sitting at my desk with two monitors and two iPads, one running the beta, one running the beta OS, which is my primary iPad because I live life on the edge, and then another iPad running the standard release because as I was writing the book, a lot of things were still broken.

450
00:34:58,640 --> 00:35:00,800
That's what I was going to ask, like when did you start this?

451
00:35:00,960 --> 00:35:08,240
Because the iOS 13 train, specifically with shortcuts as a developer who was writing an app that has shortcut support at this time.

452
00:35:09,280 --> 00:35:10,560
It was in serious flux.

453
00:35:11,280 --> 00:35:11,280
Yeah.

454
00:35:12,560 --> 00:35:17,200
So I started writing this right around the time I think I was 13 released.

455
00:35:17,280 --> 00:35:20,720
I'd have to go back and double check to make sure I'm definitely right about that.

456
00:35:20,960 --> 00:35:22,640
But I think I started it right about then.

457
00:35:24,240 --> 00:35:26,320
And I knew that things were broken.

458
00:35:26,960 --> 00:35:32,480
And of course, you know, you're filing feedback about things and talking to other people and other people like, Yeah, this is broken for me too.

459
00:35:32,560 --> 00:35:34,000
And all I've heard it's getting fixed.

460
00:35:34,080 --> 00:35:36,240
And other people are going, Yeah, I heard that's getting fixed too.

461
00:35:37,840 --> 00:35:39,280
So you're just there going, Okay.

462
00:35:40,320 --> 00:35:44,640
And the worst one is I wrote this really long shortcut about IOS 13.

463
00:35:44,640 --> 00:35:45,920
about setting your day up in the morning.

464
00:35:46,400 --> 00:35:49,120
And one of the things I was doing was iterating through calendar events.

465
00:35:49,280 --> 00:35:55,040
And the public release before the book launch broke my shortcut.

466
00:35:55,600 --> 00:36:11,280
Not broke my shortcut in a way that it wouldn't run anymore, but broke my shortcut in a way that even if you're following all my instructions, you wouldn't be able to do it because you couldn't repeat with each and use your repeat item as a calendar event, which was unsurprisingly a bit of a deal breaker.

467
00:36:12,720 --> 00:36:17,840
You know, if you're trying to iterate through a bunch of calendar events to get data out of them, you couldn't anymore.

468
00:36:19,680 --> 00:36:21,360
So, you know, I fell down.

469
00:36:21,360 --> 00:36:27,200
I felt that and just had to put a note in the book and be like, no, unfortunately, at the time of release, this is broken.

470
00:36:28,080 --> 00:36:32,240
You may be able to do it if you've had an update since I think that was 13.2.

471
00:36:33,760 --> 00:36:37,280
But we can't guarantee it, but you can always download it.

472
00:36:37,440 --> 00:36:40,640
But here are the instructions on how you would do this if this is working in shortcuts.

473
00:36:40,800 --> 00:36:45,680
But fortunately, I think that was the only thing that was broken that remained broken while it was going.

474
00:36:46,800 --> 00:36:47,760
How are you keeping up with that?

475
00:36:47,920 --> 00:36:55,920
Like, it almost seems like you'd have to have, like, in the developer world, we have regression tests that you run before you, you know, launch an update to make sure nothing's broken.

476
00:36:56,320 --> 00:37:00,080
But since Apple was changing things behind the scenes on you.

477
00:37:00,320 --> 00:37:05,600
Did you have to like go through the book and look at all your shortcuts and make sure they're all working like you were expecting?

478
00:37:05,840 --> 00:37:07,840
Well, this was the other reason for two iPads.

479
00:37:08,160 --> 00:37:16,560
I could have the shortcut that was linked in the book on one iPad and I could go through it on the other iPad and recreate it step by step.

480
00:37:16,960 --> 00:37:18,640
Ah, okay, that makes sense.

481
00:37:19,040 --> 00:37:23,280
Which is an extremely painful process to do when you're there going, I could optimize this.

482
00:37:23,520 --> 00:37:24,640
No, don't optimize things.

483
00:37:24,960 --> 00:37:26,960
You've already written three hundred words about this.

484
00:37:27,600 --> 00:37:39,120
Don't change it because then you're going to have to write another three hundred words and redo all of the screenshots and that's not insignificant amounts of work work, especially one that's already gone through the editing process and the approval process.

485
00:37:39,440 --> 00:37:40,320
You have to make it work.

486
00:37:40,800 --> 00:37:43,520
You have to basically leave it and just hope that the shortcut works.

487
00:37:43,760 --> 00:37:53,520
So yeah, I was sitting there with two iPads, you know, in the evenings most people would be doing something like, I don't know, watching Star Trek or knitting, and I'm there with two iPads, looking at the shortcut on one of them and recreating it on the other.

488
00:37:53,680 --> 00:37:55,920
My boyfriend is there going, Are you sure you're all right?

489
00:37:56,080 --> 00:37:58,080
Like you're kind of obsessive about this at the moment.

490
00:37:58,240 --> 00:37:59,200
It's like, Well, yes.

491
00:37:59,440 --> 00:38:05,120
I've got a book launch coming, and if this doesn't work as I'm talking about it, then I need to at least note that in the book for the people.

492
00:38:05,520 --> 00:38:05,600
Man.

493
00:38:06,720 --> 00:38:09,520
So then it launched in December of last year, right?

494
00:38:09,920 --> 00:38:10,080
Yeah.

495
00:38:11,040 --> 00:38:11,520
How did that go?

496
00:38:12,240 --> 00:38:25,280
That went really well, better than I was expecting, actually, because I was a little nervous about it because I'm sure a lot of the people who buy Take Control books are not necessarily the kind of people that this shortcuts would necessarily intrigue.

497
00:38:25,760 --> 00:38:30,800
A lot of their demographic are heavy Mac users and shortcuts is most decidedly not on the Mac.

498
00:38:31,920 --> 00:38:33,920
Automator is, but shortcuts is not.

499
00:38:34,480 --> 00:38:37,120
And so I was, I was nervous about it, but it went really well.

500
00:38:37,440 --> 00:38:43,120
And I had a lot of people, Automator's fans and friends saying, hey, I saw that your books were selling and I bought a copy.

501
00:38:43,200 --> 00:38:44,880
And I'm like, oh, this, this makes me feel good.

502
00:38:45,520 --> 00:38:51,760
And some of the app developers who I mentioned in the book, you know, I reached out to them to let them know and they're like, Yeah, I've already bought a copy and I'm reading it.

503
00:38:51,840 --> 00:38:52,800
It's like, Oh, okay.

504
00:38:53,520 --> 00:38:55,120
That's terrifying, but okay.

505
00:38:56,560 --> 00:39:04,960
Yeah, I basically grabbed it simply because I wanted to see like the, because you're kind of targeting people who don't already know all about it.

506
00:39:05,120 --> 00:39:05,280
Yeah.

507
00:39:05,840 --> 00:39:11,840
And I wanted to make sure that I was thinking of things the right way in terms of how normal people would look at shortcuts.

508
00:39:11,920 --> 00:39:15,520
Because I'm obviously like deep in the weeds on how the APIs and stuff work.

509
00:39:15,840 --> 00:39:18,160
And so it was really helpful as a resource just for that.

510
00:39:18,560 --> 00:39:19,040
Yeah, yeah.

511
00:39:19,280 --> 00:39:29,280
So that was the biggest difficulty for me as well because I'm so deep in this world that I don't recognize when things are really complex.

512
00:39:29,440 --> 00:39:32,800
So I was there going, okay, I really need to take a couple of steps back.

513
00:39:32,960 --> 00:39:35,200
Should I think like my mom, my dad, my grandmother?

514
00:39:35,760 --> 00:39:35,840
Yeah.

515
00:39:36,480 --> 00:39:38,320
You know, like how do I look at this?

516
00:39:38,560 --> 00:39:41,360
And that was the important part of having an editor as well.

517
00:39:41,520 --> 00:39:45,280
He was there going, like, okay, Programmer Me gets what you're talking about.

518
00:39:45,520 --> 00:39:47,600
Other Me has literally no idea.

519
00:39:48,560 --> 00:39:51,680
And you need to fix that so that Other Me completely gets it as well.

520
00:39:52,800 --> 00:39:57,040
And that, yeah, because it's so like shortcuts can be insanely complex.

521
00:39:57,120 --> 00:40:02,720
Some of the shortcuts that I have, you know, they're making HTTP requests, the things that you don't even think you could make an HTTP request to.

522
00:40:03,600 --> 00:40:05,840
There's a system at work that we used to clock in and clock out.

523
00:40:06,000 --> 00:40:09,360
It doesn't have API support, but it uses basic authentication.

524
00:40:09,600 --> 00:40:11,600
So I can send a request to it and it will clock me in.

525
00:40:12,240 --> 00:40:16,640
However, due to the way that SureCAS works, it actually returns something that says it failed.

526
00:40:17,280 --> 00:40:20,880
So I just have to know that and work with it, but I can't expect other people to know that.

527
00:40:21,680 --> 00:40:26,320
And so I was putting in, you know, I've put in things like, you know, how do you talk to the air table API?

528
00:40:27,040 --> 00:40:34,160
And I specifically wanted to include that one because the documentation for the air table API is generated based on your air table bases.

529
00:40:34,640 --> 00:40:40,000
So you go through and create a database that meets your needs, and then it creates documentation automatically that matches that.

530
00:40:40,000 --> 00:40:43,680
that matches that, which I thought for people would be really useful.

531
00:40:43,840 --> 00:40:49,520
So I used one of their template bases and basically showed you how to talk to it, giving you ideas of how you could use it.

532
00:40:50,160 --> 00:40:58,960
But still, that's a big challenge to go, okay, assuming this person doesn't know about programming at all, how do I explain this?

533
00:41:00,080 --> 00:41:01,840
Like, do I use the word iterate?

534
00:41:02,400 --> 00:41:05,520
Or do I stick with the shortcuts version, which is repeat with each?

535
00:41:05,840 --> 00:41:10,080
But if I say repeat with each ten times in the same paragraph, you might want to kill me.

536
00:41:10,480 --> 00:41:16,720
So I should probably introduce the word iterate or loop through, but then I have to explain the concept of loops.

537
00:41:17,440 --> 00:41:18,720
Yeah, so that was tricky.

538
00:41:18,880 --> 00:41:20,320
Oh, that's fascinating.

539
00:41:20,880 --> 00:41:35,120
It kind of is, it reminds me of designing an app in the sense that, like, you're trying to guide people through something and you can get people to do things that are very complex, but you have to, like, guide them through that.

540
00:41:35,280 --> 00:41:40,320
So, like, you were just saying, you can use the word loop, but then first you have to sort of explain what that is.

541
00:41:40,480 --> 00:41:40,560
Yeah.

542
00:41:40,960 --> 00:41:47,680
And I've never really thought of that from, like, a book writing perspective, but you're sort of taking people on this journey through this book.

543
00:41:48,320 --> 00:41:50,640
And, ah, that's, yeah, that's really interesting.

544
00:41:51,120 --> 00:41:55,760
So, do you have, like, sort of user profiles of, like, who you are're targeting?

545
00:41:56,480 --> 00:42:04,720
No, I don't use the stories to, but yeah, but that is the kind of thing though, isn't it?

546
00:42:05,280 --> 00:42:09,920
It's the same with the agile method, you know, like, okay, so what is this person looking to achieve and how can they do that?

547
00:42:10,800 --> 00:42:16,000
I didn't really have that, but I did have people in mind for my sample shortcuts.

548
00:42:16,880 --> 00:42:24,720
You know, like who is the kind of person that might use this and things like that, so that I would know, you know, what they were trying to achieve.

549
00:42:24,880 --> 00:42:33,680
And that was the other challenge, coming up with sample shortcuts that both were not inane, but also were easy to understand or easy enough to understand.

550
00:42:34,800 --> 00:42:36,880
Trying to capture that balance was quite tricky.

551
00:42:37,280 --> 00:42:56,160
Yeah, it's kind of an amorphous thing to try and wrap your head around if you're not already familiar with scripting as a concept, which is who target or who shortcuts is kind of almost built to address is like bringing these people in who don't necessarily want to, you know, write scripting code necessarily, but do it in a user friendly way.

552
00:42:56,400 --> 00:43:03,600
And the shortcuts team and the workflow team before did an amazing job and still have a really difficult job targeting that group of people.

553
00:43:04,960 --> 00:43:07,920
But you sort of had to inherit that same problem space, I guess.

554
00:43:09,200 --> 00:43:14,480
Basically, it's one of those things, you know, like, oh, wow, is this person actually going to use it?

555
00:43:14,640 --> 00:43:22,720
And some of the feedback I've got, you know, I had an example event template in there and someone said, well, I'm not going to use that because I already have to look at my calendar.

556
00:43:22,960 --> 00:43:26,320
And if I'm already in my calendar, then I'm just going to add the event right there.

557
00:43:26,400 --> 00:43:33,520
And it's like, yes, but if you have to add, you know, specific addresses and notes and stuff like that, you know, you're going to make a mistake.

558
00:43:33,680 --> 00:43:51,040
So you, you missed the part at the start of the book where I said, well, it's not just about speeding things up, it's about the accuracy, which is the other part that I was trying to keep in mind when I was, explaining these things to people, you know, like some of the times to use automation is not because it's faster, it's because it's more accurate.

559
00:43:51,520 --> 00:43:54,240
And I think that was a selling point for a lot of people.

560
00:43:54,640 --> 00:44:00,160
So have you heard from people that have read the book that it kind of brought them into the shortcuts world at all?

561
00:44:00,640 --> 00:44:01,520
Yes, yes, I have.

562
00:44:01,680 --> 00:44:11,120
A lot of people have told me, Oh, yes, you know, like, there's a book I can actually watch it, you know, I can actually get it because, of course, David Sparks had his field guide, a video field guide, and that came out.

563
00:44:11,840 --> 00:44:15,440
And so we've got a little bit of friendly competition going on there between the automated guys.

564
00:44:16,160 --> 00:44:17,840
But, you know, a book is very different.

565
00:44:18,160 --> 00:44:21,200
And I have always been much more a book person than a video person.

566
00:44:22,000 --> 00:44:32,960
If I google how to do something and there's a ten minute YouTube video and a two thousand word article, I will pick the two thousand word article every time because I can just scroll past the parts that don't interest me.

567
00:44:33,200 --> 00:44:38,560
But trying to skim past the parts that don't interest me in a YouTube video is so much more difficult.

568
00:44:39,760 --> 00:44:44,720
And I think a lot of people feel that way in many respects and some people are like, Oh my gosh, two thousand word article.

569
00:44:45,600 --> 00:44:48,400
No, I'd much rather watch a video and that's totally okay.

570
00:44:48,560 --> 00:44:54,800
And you know, if they think that's the case, I'm very happy for them to buy David's field guide because that clearly will be better for them.

571
00:44:55,840 --> 00:44:58,960
And you know, I also made a point of, you know, well, David's field guide exists.

572
00:44:59,520 --> 00:45:07,200
I am not going to copy any of his shortcuts because then for the people that have got both, they've got different, completely different, you know, results out of this.

573
00:45:07,360 --> 00:45:08,320
They've got two different things.

574
00:45:08,560 --> 00:45:11,600
But I've heard from a lot of people that, you know, they've got it now.

575
00:45:12,400 --> 00:45:25,120
And they've been asking for other resources where they can, you know, see other sample shortcuts and that's always lovely to be able to point them towards Max's stories and David and Matthew Casanelli as well to give them other places where they can get good things.

576
00:45:25,760 --> 00:45:25,840
Yeah.

577
00:45:26,000 --> 00:45:30,640
That's that's got to be like a cool feeling to kind of guide people into this world.

578
00:45:30,800 --> 00:45:35,680
I guess it's it's a similar thing to like you said you wanted to teach any teaching.

579
00:45:36,320 --> 00:45:43,360
position is sort of getting to bring people into a world and if you can explain it to them in a way that they get, it could literally change their life.

580
00:45:43,600 --> 00:45:47,680
I mean, that sounds a little, you know, extreme, but you know what I mean?

581
00:45:47,840 --> 00:45:53,120
Like having an automation mindset can definitely change how you live your whole life.

582
00:45:53,520 --> 00:45:56,240
Yes, it definitely can and even if it just makes things easier.

583
00:45:56,400 --> 00:46:00,720
So my dad doesn't realize it, but he uses shortcuts almost every day because he has a blood glucose monitor.

584
00:46:01,040 --> 00:46:01,840
He's not diabetic.

585
00:46:02,000 --> 00:46:07,440
He just checks his blood sugar to make sure it's still within normal boundaries because he has had problems with it before.

586
00:46:08,240 --> 00:46:10,720
And he wanted to log this data into the health application.

587
00:46:11,200 --> 00:46:12,640
Well, anyone who has tried to use this.

588
00:46:12,800 --> 00:46:15,280
this manually, logging data into the health application.

589
00:46:15,760 --> 00:46:17,040
It's a bit of a nightmare.

590
00:46:17,360 --> 00:46:19,520
So I created a shortcut that's on his home screen.

591
00:46:19,920 --> 00:46:23,760
And when he sees the blood glucose monitor, it's there and it works for him.

592
00:46:24,240 --> 00:46:27,920
And that's the kind of thing that, you know, it hasn't necessarily changed his life.

593
00:46:28,240 --> 00:46:35,760
But it meant when it means when he goes to the doctor, that he can pull out his phone and show the doctor this graph and the doctor can look at it and go, Well, that's fine.

594
00:46:36,240 --> 00:46:37,360
You know, there's nothing wrong there.

595
00:46:37,760 --> 00:46:38,880
I'm totally happy with that.

596
00:46:39,920 --> 00:46:42,160
And so, you know, he's now less stressed.

597
00:46:42,640 --> 00:46:52,480
And I, you know, it's the small wins like that that I think that everybody really ought to be looking for when it comes to automation because those are the ones that are really going to change your life for the better.

598
00:46:53,440 --> 00:46:56,560
Just getting rid of that tiny little bit of aggravation every time.

599
00:46:57,040 --> 00:47:18,640
So one thing I wanted to ask you, just because this is like a world that you seem to understand extremely well is this year, I was going to say at WWDC, but whatever it is that ends up happening where Apple introduces iOS 14, what would you like to see in the shortcuts or just general automation space coming out of them?

600
00:47:19,680 --> 00:47:25,440
That is really tricky to see because some of what I want is actually more hardware based.

601
00:47:25,600 --> 00:47:31,280
So I would like to see them actually roll out a keyboard with a trackpad, which thanks to Mac Rumors, we think is almost certainly coming.

602
00:47:31,600 --> 00:47:33,760
Yeah, yeah, that's fresh off the rumor mail.

603
00:47:34,080 --> 00:47:34,160
Yeah.

604
00:47:35,360 --> 00:47:38,960
And the other thing that I would like to see is better podcasting support for iOS.

605
00:47:39,760 --> 00:47:48,960
Now, I know I'm being extremely selfish here because for the vast majority of people, this will make absolutely zero difference being able to specify the audio inputs and outputs on your device.

606
00:47:49,920 --> 00:47:56,560
And it should take, you know, that all applications should use this and that I can use two different applications with the microphone at the same time.

607
00:47:57,360 --> 00:48:07,280
But I think that that will, you know, unleash a whole wave of creativity for people because right now., a lot of people who work with audio can't use iOS really.

608
00:48:07,440 --> 00:48:17,360
I mean, you've got Fairrite and a few other things which really do make working with audio on iPad or iPhone great, but a lot of people can't actually record on it.

609
00:48:17,680 --> 00:48:19,680
And I feel that that's kind of missing.

610
00:48:20,880 --> 00:48:25,680
And of course, automation wise, I am looking to see what happens next with shortcuts.

611
00:48:26,320 --> 00:48:38,400
I'm hoping that we're going to get more integration, both, you know, taking out the currently in most cases outdated system shortcut actions that are there.

612
00:48:38,560 --> 00:48:40,480
So, for example, Omni Focus of recently.

613
00:48:40,960 --> 00:48:46,880
added their own actions, but the original Omni Focus actions are still there and they're actually broken at the first summary.

614
00:48:47,600 --> 00:48:51,280
So I know a lot of app developers have seen this over the years, you know, and you have to contact the Shortcuts team.

615
00:48:51,440 --> 00:48:54,720
And by the way, can you update this or can you replace this or can you remove this?

616
00:48:55,360 --> 00:48:58,320
And that's tied to the release cycles and that's great for the Shortcuts team.

617
00:48:58,640 --> 00:49:02,960
I'm really excited for them that Shortcuts is shipping directly on iPadOS.

618
00:49:03,440 --> 00:49:10,160
I'm just hoping that the gallery gets a little bit of an improvement because I've noticed recently some shortcuts that you can download from the gallery are broken.

619
00:49:11,040 --> 00:49:11,360
Oh man.

620
00:49:11,760 --> 00:49:20,720
So I've been filing reports on these and I would encourage anyone else who downloads a broker shortcut from the Shortcuts gallery in in the shortcuts app to report that as well with feedback to Apple.

621
00:49:21,840 --> 00:49:32,960
But that, you know, I'm hoping that they can automatically create shortcuts, not just create suggestions for shortcuts, but they will start actually automatically creating shortcuts.

622
00:49:33,120 --> 00:49:40,640
And they will let people add multiple phrases to one shortcut to trigger it through the information interface, things like that.

623
00:49:41,040 --> 00:49:54,400
That would be really interesting to see because I think a lot of people don't quite understand right now that if they want to add, you know, if they want three different ways to trigger a shortcut with their voice, then well, you don't duplicate your shortcut or recreate your shortcut.

624
00:49:54,880 --> 00:49:59,520
or you add another shortcut and you use the run shortcut action, but that doesn't feel intuitive.

625
00:50:00,640 --> 00:50:08,640
And I'm hoping that the intuitiveness will improve and that my English will probably improve so that I don't create as many words as intuitiveness.

626
00:50:10,400 --> 00:50:16,480
Yeah, I secretly am hoping that they expand on the sort of what do they call them?

627
00:50:16,640 --> 00:50:22,240
Automations within shortcuts, which is a weird word, but the things where it can be triggered like sort of in the background.

628
00:50:23,520 --> 00:50:30,800
Specifically, I wish you could run a shortcut that is allowed to run in the background from a timer without the user having to interact with it.

629
00:50:31,040 --> 00:50:32,720
Yes, that would be awesome.

630
00:50:32,960 --> 00:50:41,840
Because yeah, everyone asks me for like me to add a timer function where you can set a sound to kick off at a certain point in the morning or whatever.

631
00:50:42,320 --> 00:50:49,680
And there's really weird hacks to do that in iOS, but it's not really a thing that we have the ability to do.

632
00:50:50,000 --> 00:50:50,080
No.

633
00:50:50,480 --> 00:50:57,520
But if you could make a shortcut, then you could just set an alarm and I have enough shortcut support that I would just kinda get that for free.

634
00:50:57,680 --> 00:50:58,160
So yeah.

635
00:50:58,400 --> 00:50:59,440
Fingers crossed on that one.

636
00:50:59,680 --> 00:51:06,640
Well, I'm hoping that at the very least that they're going to give us an action called run shortcut when.

637
00:51:07,840 --> 00:51:13,840
Um, so that then you can say, okay, like right now, I'm going to schedule this shortcut to run at this time.

638
00:51:14,080 --> 00:51:18,720
So then when you turn your alarm off in the morning, it can say, okay, and at 10 o'clock start playing dark noise.

639
00:51:19,200 --> 00:51:24,480
Um, and maybe we'll be able to get rid of the notifications that will say, well, you know, I'm running your shortcut now it's 10 a.m.

640
00:51:24,640 --> 00:51:27,360
and you asked me to do this at 8:30 when your alarm went off.

641
00:51:27,600 --> 00:51:36,320
But, you know, if they could give us an action that says, okay, well, wait this, you know, X period of time or when I get to this location today, let me do this.

642
00:51:36,480 --> 00:51:41,360
So maybe, you know, we'll have to build this into other automations, but I think that that would be pretty nice.

643
00:51:42,000 --> 00:51:42,080
Yeah.

644
00:51:42,400 --> 00:51:43,040
Yeah, definitely.

645
00:51:43,920 --> 00:51:53,120
Um, so I'm going to wrap up by asking the question that I ask everyone on the show, which is, what is a person or people out there that have inspired you that you recommend other people follow?

646
00:51:53,840 --> 00:52:06,320
Well, I mean, I feel like I'm kind of cheating here because I would have to go with David Sparks because without David, I wouldn't have started Automatoes and I don't think I would be where I am today.

647
00:52:07,840 --> 00:52:10,160
But a lot of people are already following David.

648
00:52:10,800 --> 00:52:13,760
So I'm going to go with the second person as well.

649
00:52:14,720 --> 00:52:22,560
And that is actually Scotty Jackson, my co-host at Nested Builders, because he's really giving me different ways to look at shortcuts and automation.

650
00:52:23,520 --> 00:52:27,520
He doesn't consider himself a professional automator, unlike myself.

651
00:52:28,400 --> 00:52:34,240
And it's really interesting to see what he comes up with and how he changes things and creates things that work for him.

652
00:52:35,520 --> 00:52:38,560
So, yeah, to people for people if they're not already following David.

653
00:52:39,280 --> 00:52:56,000
Yeah, and that, that, like, different perspective is always really nice because once you become a power user, this is the thing that, like, Mike Hurley and Federico Viticci were talking about on the latest connected where everyone's talking about their opinions on how iPad multitasking should work.

654
00:52:56,400 --> 00:53:00,800
And they're kind of like they're trying to step out of their expertise on how all that works.

655
00:53:01,120 --> 00:53:07,200
And, you know, it's kind of hard to have that perspective of, like, a normal is kind of a weird word to use.

656
00:53:07,360 --> 00:53:07,920
But you know what I mean?

657
00:53:08,160 --> 00:53:14,000
A person who, like, doesn't live and breathe in this space to see how they, how they use these tools that you know so well.

658
00:53:14,480 --> 00:53:15,200
Yes, absolutely.

659
00:53:15,520 --> 00:53:19,200
And I, I showed my mom multitasking on her iPad a couple of weekends ago.

660
00:53:19,760 --> 00:53:22,400
And she didn't know that this feature existed.

661
00:53:23,920 --> 00:53:26,160
Which I think says pretty much everything about that.

662
00:53:26,560 --> 00:53:30,160
But at the same time, I asked her how she would want multitasking to work.

663
00:53:30,320 --> 00:53:32,480
Because I was like, okay, this is perfect, Joshua.

664
00:53:32,720 --> 00:53:34,080
She had no idea this exists.

665
00:53:34,480 --> 00:53:39,840
And she was like, well, I guess the way it works is kind of logical, but I wish they'd told me about it.

666
00:53:40,880 --> 00:53:43,920
So yeah, so just like some sort of discoverability or something.

667
00:53:44,480 --> 00:53:56,240
Yeah, so yeah, so I don't know what the answer is on that one either, but finding different perspectives and getting other people to talk you through these things is always useful, and that's one of the many things that Scotty is good at for me.

668
00:53:56,640 --> 00:53:56,960
Awesome.

669
00:53:57,200 --> 00:53:59,600
Well, thank you so much, Rosemary, for coming on.

670
00:53:59,760 --> 00:54:00,800
This is really exciting.

671
00:54:01,200 --> 00:54:02,400
Well, thank you very much for having me.

672
00:54:02,560 --> 00:54:10,160
It was lovely to actually get to talk to you because I've been recommending your app for a long time to a lot of people, so I'm glad to actually talk to the creator.

673
00:54:10,320 --> 00:54:12,000
Oh yeah, I guess I should thank you in person.

674
00:54:13,040 --> 00:54:17,280
You gave me a like award on the suite setup, right?

675
00:54:17,760 --> 00:54:26,880
Yes, I was an assistant in getting you that award because we all had to nominate things and I I got in early so I got to nominate Dark Noise.

676
00:54:27,200 --> 00:54:33,840
Well, I can't tell you how excited I was whenever I started getting the tweets that morning, so thank you very much for that.

677
00:54:34,160 --> 00:54:43,680
Well, I'm very glad to be able to recommend it because it's so nice to see both new applications that actually look good and work and that have automation support.

678
00:54:44,000 --> 00:54:47,360
Because a lot of people will be like, why do you need automation support in a white noise application?

679
00:54:47,520 --> 00:54:51,120
I'm like, oh, I could tell you all of the things.

680
00:54:52,800 --> 00:55:00,160
Well, and like the cool thing with Apple is once you start supporting something, you just start getting this long tree of things that you get for free.

681
00:55:00,400 --> 00:55:00,480
Yes.

682
00:55:01,120 --> 00:55:14,720
I can't tell you how many times I get tweets from people that Apple's automated recommendation system will just pop up a thing on their notification center that says, hey, do you want to start this sound right as they're going to bed?

683
00:55:14,960 --> 00:55:18,080
Because because I just happen to support those APIs, I got all that for free.

684
00:55:18,160 --> 00:55:26,800
So any developers out there, there's a composing interest whenever it comes to adding automation support or any of those like APIs that Apple really pushes.

685
00:55:26,960 --> 00:55:27,040
Yeah.

686
00:55:27,200 --> 00:55:35,680
Yeah, I really need to add that to WemWorks at some point, but I've only just taken it over and I'm still digging through the code base trying to figure out, do I actually understand Objective C?

687
00:55:36,800 --> 00:55:41,920
Yeah, the answer is no, I don't really understand Objective C, so I'm working on updating things without breaking things first.

688
00:55:42,880 --> 00:55:46,400
Well, I'm in the same boat as you, so we can share that.

